# Married to the Don to Keep Me Silent - Narrated Film (2 speakers)
<!-- SOURCE OF TRUTH. Parsed by ofs/adaptation.py. Format:
## CHnn | Title            chapter (YouTube chapter marker)
@sNN | motion | characters | location | camera | action | continuity   one shot (segments after it belong to it)
> note                       direction for the NEXT line (tone/pacing/audio); never spoken
H: text {p=0.4}              Heba (Ivy narration; also quotes every non-Nico character)
L: text {p=0.4}              Lion (Nico's dialogue and Nico-POV narration)
{p=...} = pause after in seconds.  Lines with [CTA] are the optional book mention. -->

## CH00 | Two Shots
@s01 | zoom_in | frankie,nico | garage | low wide, eye level behind a car bumper | Level two of a concrete hospital garage at 4:10 a.m.; a man in a good dark coat slumped down the side of a silver sedan, a pistol on the wet floor a foot from his open palm; a tall man stands over him | no blood visible; garage lights orange; floor wet; Frankie's face turned away
> Cold open. Close, low, almost a whisper. No music. One room-tone hum.
H: Two shots. That's all it took. {p=0.6}
H: Ten past four that Friday morning, crouched behind my own bumper in a hospital garage, I learned what a life ending sounds like in concrete. {p=0.4}
@s02 | pan_right | nico,frankie | garage | medium, slow pan from the fallen man's hand to the standing man | Nico stands over the fallen man holding a pistol low in his LEFT hand; his RIGHT forearm is in a white plaster splint held against his chest; a second gun lies near the open hand | LEFT-hand gun, RIGHT-arm white plaster splint; gray eyes; scar LEFT eyebrow
H: I looked over the hood. A man was sliding down the side of a silver sedan, a gun a foot from his open hand. The man standing over him held a different gun, in his left hand. His right arm was in the plaster splint I had built thirty minutes earlier, in bay fourteen, with my own two hands. {p=0.8}
@s03 | zoom_in | nico | garage | close-up, ivy point of view | Nico looks straight into the lens, calm and tired, as if he had expected her and was sorry to be right | scar LEFT eyebrow; gray eyes; dark wavy hair; stubble; wet orange light on one side of his face
> Silence for two full seconds before the line. Low, quiet, no threat in it.
L: Go back inside, Dr. Marlowe. You were never here. {p=1.8}
@s04 | zoom_out | ivy,nico | garage | wide, high angle | Ivy in navy scrubs standing small in the doorway between concrete pillars, the tall man far behind her in orange light, wet floor between them | emerald wedding ring is NOT on the chain yet - she is not married yet
H: I went back inside. Three days later I married him. This is how a Brooklyn don became my husband to keep me silent, and who was really on those stairs. Stay to the end and you'll hear one sentence I swore to under oath, the one I had never said to him. {p=1.0}

## CH01 | 3:05
@s05 | zoom_in | priya | er | medium wide through the glass of the nurses' station | A crowded Brooklyn ER at night, stretchers lining the hall, Priya at the glass-walled nurses' station with an energy drink the color of antifreeze, looking toward the doors | Priya tall, scrubs, tired humor
> Light, quick, funny. Let the first beat smile.
H: It started at 3:05 that morning, with a man walking into my ER holding his own arm closed. Half the waiting room went quiet for him. Denise at triage half stood up. The security guard found something interesting on his shoes. Everybody knew a rule about this man. Nobody had told me. {p=0.4}
H: Bay nine had sung its fourth "Livin' on a Prayer." My friend Priya set down her energy drink. Priya never sets down the can. {p=0.4}
@s06 | none | ivy,nico | er | medium, hallway | Nico walks past triage in a ruined charcoal suit, his left hand clamped around his right forearm, blood dripping between his fingers; Ivy pulls a pen from her hair knot and walks toward him alone | Ivy: red messy bun with a pen, freckles, navy scrubs; no ring; Nico: dark wavy hair, stubble, gray eyes, scar LEFT eyebrow
H: He came past triage without hurrying. Tall, black hair, a suit that cost more than my car, now mostly ruined. One drop of blood a second. Not an artery. Still too much. I pulled the pen out of my hair and walked toward him. Nobody else did. {p=0.5}
@s07 | zoom_in | ivy,nico | er | close two-shot over his forearm | Bay 14 under a buzzing light: Ivy sutures a long clean cut on the inside of his right forearm; his gray eyes watch her hands, then flick to a wall clock | Nico wears no splint yet; scar LEFT eyebrow; Ivy gloves, red bun with pen
H: Bay fourteen. A clean cut across the inside of his right forearm. Gray eyes. A thin white scar through his left eyebrow. {p=0.3}
H: "Knife?" I asked. {p=0.2}
L: I'd rather not say what cut it. {p=0.3}
H: That's a yes. Under the wound, the tendon to his ring finger was cut more than halfway through. At 3:20 I put one stitch into the tendon itself and eleven in the skin. Twice his eyes went to the clock. {p=0.3}
H: "Somewhere you need to be?" {p=0.3}
L: You've done this before. {p=0.3}
H: "A couple hundred times. Usually on people who tell me their name." {p=0.4}
L: Nico. {p=0.5}
L: Vescari. {p=0.5}
@s08 | zoom_out | ivy,nico | er | medium, the instrument tray in foreground | A fold of hundred-dollar bills lies on a steel instrument tray beside bloody gauze; Nico in a fresh white plaster splint from fingertips to just under the elbow, wrist tipped down; Ivy tucking the money back into his jacket pocket | white plaster splint RIGHT arm; scar LEFT eyebrow; computer screen showing his name
H: By 3:40 I had built him a splint of plaster, fingertips to elbow. Six weeks, no gripping. If he grabbed anything hard, that tendon would snap like a rubber band. He set a fold of hundreds on my tray. {p=0.3}
L: For the chart. There doesn't need to be one. {p=0.4}
H: "There's always a chart." I tucked the money back into his pocket and typed his name while he watched. I said it too hard. He couldn't know why. {p=0.5}
L: Dottoressa. {p=0.3}
H: "Is that a compliment?" {p=0.3}
L: It's a title. Thank you, Dr. Marlowe. {p=0.8}

## CH02 | 4:10
@s09 | none | ivy | garage | low, from behind a parked car | Ivy ducked behind her own hatchback on level two with a phone charger in her fist, arms over her head, orange concrete lights, wet floor | navy scrubs; red bun with pen; no ring; spot 2-14
> Pace quickens. Footsteps and one heavy thump in the sound design, nothing else.
H: Level two, spot 2-14. At 4:08 I found the charger in the cup holder. At 4:10, two shots went off, so close together they sounded like one word. I counted. One, two, three, four. Footsteps. Something heavy hitting a car door. At nine, I looked. {p=0.6}
@s10 | zoom_in | ivy,nico,frankie | garage | medium wide from Ivy's hiding place | Ivy comes around the car with her hands out, ready to work; Frankie on the ground breathing wetly; Nico steps one pace left to put himself between them | Nico gun in LEFT hand; RIGHT arm in white plaster splint at his chest; Frankie two small holes in the shirt, no gore
H: The man on the ground made a wet, bubbling sound. I knew that sound. Air and blood in the chest, a lung filling. He was alive. Did I think about the gun? I'd like to say I did. I stood up and went toward him with my hands out, my brain a full step behind, screaming. {p=0.3}
H: "Let me get to him," I said. "I can—" {p=0.3}
L: He's gone, Dottoressa. Go. {p=0.6}
@s11 | zoom_in | ivy | garage | tight close-up | Ivy frozen with her empty hands raised, counting, orange light on her face; behind her a stairwell door | red bun with pen; freckles; navy scrubs; tears not falling
H: Behind him, in the stairwell, feet were going down fast. Then they stopped. Someone was standing on those stairs, listening, and those stairs were my only way out. I counted his breaths. Seven. Eight. Nine. Ten. The next one didn't come. The last time I stood that close to someone dying while other people decided I should stay out of the way, I was fourteen. {p=0.8}
@s12 | pan_left | ivy | er | medium | Ivy sliding down the staff bathroom door with a phone to her ear, a steady face in the mirror and shaking legs | navy scrubs; pen in hair knot
H: At 4:16 I called 911 and my voice came out steady. By 4:31 the silver sedan was gone, and so was the man. The floor smelled like bleach and fake pine. The camera had been down since Tuesday. A young officer told me a car backfiring sounds like a cannon in concrete. {p=0.4}
H: I had a name on my tongue. I didn't say it. I'm not proud of that. {p=0.9}

## CH03 | What I Saw, And What I Didn't
@s13 | none | ivy,reyes,holloway | ivy_apt | medium, living room | Saturday morning, Ivy in sweatpants and a Mets T-shirt standing while an FBI agent in a rain jacket and a prosecutor in a navy suit sit on her worn couch; a subpoena on the coffee table | Reyes rain jacket, rumpled; Holloway navy suit, good teeth
H: Saturday at eight, a woman in a rain jacket and a man in a navy suit sat on my couch. Special Agent Reyes. Prosecutor Holloway. A city crew had pulled a man out of the Gowanus Canal. Frankie Lombardi. Two gunshot wounds to the chest. Frankie worked for the Vescari family, and also, quietly, for the FBI. That made his murder a federal case, and me its witness. {p=0.4}
H: I told them in order, like presenting a patient. Two shots at 4:10. Nico standing over a man by a silver car, a gun in his left hand, my splint on his right. Notice what I didn't say: that I saw anyone fire. I heard two shots. I saw what came after. {p=0.5}
H: "The gun was in his left hand," I said. Reyes looked up from her notebook. Holloway didn't. {p=0.4}
@s14 | zoom_in | ivy,holloway | ivy_apt | close-up on the subpoena | A grand jury subpoena with IVY CLAIRE MARLOWE typed on it, a business card with a cell number set on top | none
H: Holloway left a subpoena. Thursday, ten a.m. He offered a new name, a new city, witness protection. I laughed. I had nine months of residency left. I wasn't becoming a dental hygienist in Tucson because a man in a nice suit told me to go back inside. {p=0.3}
H: "I'll tell them," I said. "But I'm doing it as myself." {p=0.8}

## CH04 | Two Ways This Ends
@s15 | zoom_in | ivy,nico,theo | ivy_apt | medium wide, dim | Evening: Ivy in the doorway with grocery bags; Nico in a dark sweater sitting in her late mother's armchair in an unlit living room, white splint resting on the chair arm; Theo pale on the couch with his phone gripped in both hands | white plaster splint RIGHT arm; Ivy red bun with pen
> Quiet menace without raising the voice. Let Lion be unhurried.
H: At seven that night I carried groceries up three flights and found my door unlocked and my lamp off. Nico Vescari sat in my mother's armchair in the dark, my splint resting on the arm. My little brother sat beside him, white as paper. "Ivy," Theo said. "I'm so sorry." {p=0.5}
H: Forty thousand dollars, a betting app, then interest. Theo was crying the way he did at fifteen, when he thought I couldn't hear through the wall. {p=0.4}
L: His bookie works for Carmine Brancato, who wants my port. If you die before Thursday, with my name in your statement, nobody needs a trial. {p=0.5}
H: "So someone wants me dead, and it isn't you." {p=0.3}
L: It isn't me. {p=0.3}
H: "Did you kill that man?" {p=0.3}
L: I'm not going to answer that. {p=0.3}
H: "You say that a lot." {p=0.3}
L: I don't lie. So yes. I say it a lot. {p=0.4}
@s16 | zoom_out | ivy,nico | ivy_apt | tight two-shot | Nico stands by the window; Ivy holds up the subpoena so he can read her name; the R train lights flicker across both faces | Nico splint RIGHT arm; scar LEFT eyebrow
L: Marry me. A wife can't be forced to testify against her husband. The privilege belongs to the wife. Not to me. Not to Holloway. {p=0.4}
H: I laughed. "I met you on Friday. You bled on my shoes. Here's my answer." I held up the subpoena. "I'd rather testify." {p=0.5}
H: He set a black card on the arm of my mother's chair. No name. Just a number. {p=0.3}
L: When you change your mind. {p=0.3}
H: "If." {p=0.2}
L: When, Dottoressa. {p=0.6}
@s17 | none | ivy,rocco | er | medium, dim supply room | A narrow supply room, shelves to the ceiling, a rolling cart, boxes of gauze spilled across the floor; Ivy against the cart with trauma shears in her fist and a red line around her throat; a huge calm man in the doorway | red line on throat; navy scrubs; Rocco huge, quiet, folded newspaper under his arm
> Fast and tight. This is the one frightening beat. Then silence.
H: Sunday, a man in bay seventeen asked for the redheaded doctor. In the supply room the door clicked shut and IV tubing dropped over my head. You get about ten seconds before you pass out. I teach that to students. At five I drove my trauma shears into the back of his hand, and he ran. {p=0.4}
H: A huge man filled the doorway, the quiet one with the crossword. Rocco. "Go after him," I croaked. {p=0.3}
H: "No, ma'am. My job's you." {p=0.5}
H: That evening I stood by my car with a red line round my throat. I could become someone else in a city I'd never seen, or keep my work and my brother and trust a man I didn't. I was scared of him. I was more scared of a plastic tube. He answered on the first ring. {p=0.4}
H: "I have conditions," I said. {p=1.0}

## CH05 | Terms
@s18 | zoom_in | ivy,nico,tommy | restaurant | wide, long table | The back room of a closed Italian restaurant at night, dark wood walls with old photographs of men in hats; Ivy in an oversized gray cardigan facing Nico at the head of a long table; a gray-haired lawyer in a bow tie with four pens lined up, and a buzz-cut man arms crossed | cardigan sleeves pulled over her hands; red bun with pen; red mark on throat; Nico white shirt, sleeve rolled over the white splint
> Quick business rhythm. Let the humor land, keep moving.
H: His lawyer wrote it down in blue ink. One. I keep my residency, every shift. Two. My own bedroom, with a lock on my side. Three. Theo's debt, gone tonight. Four. No one in his family touches me, including his men. Five. When the case is dead, we divorce. Six. Not one cent of his money. {p=0.3}
H: "Most people ask for the opposite," the lawyer said. {p=0.2}
H: "Most people didn't watch him try to pay for a chart." {p=0.4}
H: "And seven. You never lie to me." {p=0.4}
@s19 | zoom_in | ivy,nico | restaurant | close, over the lawyer's legal pad | Nico looks at Ivy for a long moment; the buzz-cut man looks down at the table as if he knows the answer to her next question | scar LEFT eyebrow; splint RIGHT arm
L: I'll never lie to you. I won't always answer. {p=0.4}
H: "That's a loophole." {p=0.2}
L: That's the truth. You asked for the truth. {p=0.4}
H: "Is it the only reason you want this? If I can't testify, you're safe?" {p=0.3}
L: No. {p=0.4}
H: "What's the other one?" {p=0.3}
H: He held my eyes and said nothing at all. Beside him, Tommy looked at the table. Whatever it was, Tommy knew. {p=0.8}
@s20 | pan_right | ivy,nico,lucia | restaurant | medium, doorway | A small woman in a black buttoned dress with a gold cross at her collarbone and silver-streaked hair pulled tight stands in the doorway; every man at the table stands; Ivy stands too | Lucia black dress, gold cross; Ivy cardigan; Nico splint RIGHT arm
H: The door opened. A small woman in black, a gold cross at her collarbone. Every man stood, even Tommy. "So," she said, in a thick Sicilian accent. "This is the rat you're bringing into my kitchen." {p=0.4}
H: "I'm a doctor, Mrs. Vescari. Rats don't do sutures." {p=0.5}
H: Her eyes went to her son's arm, my splint, and back to me. She said something short and sharp in Italian and left. {p=0.3}
H: Outside, I asked Nico what she'd said. {p=0.3}
L: She said you have a mouth. {p=0.3}
H: "And what did you say?" {p=0.2}
L: I said, I know. {p=0.8}

## CH06 | The Code (Nico)
@s21 | zoom_in | nico | study | close, desk lamp | Nico alone at a dark green leather desk in his father's study, trying to close the fingers of his splinted right hand; only his thumb and first finger curl | white plaster splint RIGHT arm; scar LEFT eyebrow; stubble
> Switch to Lion as the narrator. Lower, drier, more guarded than Heba. Quiet room tone.
L: I lasted three days before I tried to make a fist. My thumb came in. My first finger came in. The other two stopped halfway, and pain ran up to my elbow. Even in the garage she had come around that car with her hands already out. I'd had to step in front of a dying man to stop her. {p=0.5}
@s22 | none | nico,enzo | study | medium, doorway | A twenty-one-year-old with gold curls in a gray hoodie hesitates in the study doorway, red-rimmed eyes, not coming farther in | Enzo gold curls, thin, gray hoodie
L: Enzo came in barefoot, twenty-one, with our mother's gold curls. He hadn't laughed since Friday. "Nico. If she ever—" {p=0.3}
L: Go to bed, Enzo. You were never there. {p=0.5}
L: He nodded like a boy sent to his room. Under the desk, my left hand closed on my knee hard enough to hurt. {p=0.5}
@s23 | zoom_in | nico,sal | study | medium, leather couch | An older man with warm brown eyes and silver hair in a camel coat sits on the leather couch under a portrait, a white bakery box of cannoli open on the desk, powdered sugar on his tie | Sal silver hair, warm smile, white pastry box; Nico stands with splint RIGHT arm
L: My uncle Sal came at one-thirty with a box of cannoli and sugar on his tie. My father's brother, his consigliere for twenty-five years, and now mine. {p=0.3}
L: "You're a better man than your father was," Sal said. "But a better man makes trouble for himself. A girl in the house with a federal subpoena. It would be cleaner, Nico, the other way." {p=0.4}
L: We don't touch civilians. That's the only thing that makes us different from Brancato. Take that away and we're just men with guns who like the port. {p=0.5}
L: He held up both hands, cannoli in one. "I had to say it so you could say no. That's my job." Then he went out to the garden for air and left the door open behind him. {p=0.5}
@s24 | zoom_out | nico | study | medium, over his shoulder | Nico at the dark window looking down at the garden with lemon trees in clay pots, a man in a coat standing by the back wall | none
L: No drugs. No women. No kids. Keep your word. Never talk to the government. And you don't put a doctor in the water because she wrote your name down. I had known her for forty minutes, and she had said my name to a computer. {p=1.0}

## CH07 | I Do
@s25 | zoom_in | ivy,priya,theo | courthouse | medium, judge's chambers | Monday 8:52 a.m. in a judge's wood-paneled chambers, a candy bowl of butterscotch, Priya in a yellow sundress whispering to Ivy, Theo in a too-long borrowed navy tie with gold anchors | Priya yellow sundress, jean jacket; Theo thin, tie too long; Ivy navy dress, red bun, concealer over the throat mark
H: Monday, 8:52 a.m., eight minutes before I married a man I'd seen standing over a body, I was unwrapping my third butterscotch from the judge's candy bowl because my hands needed a job. Priya whispered, "Blink twice if this is a hostage situation." Theo wore a tie borrowed from Rocco that hung past his belt. I fixed his knot. {p=0.4}
@s26 | zoom_in | ivy,nico | courthouse | close on the contract page | The last page of an eleven-page agreement; under the typed lines, big shaky left-handed capital letters in blue ink reading SHE WILL LOOK LIKE SHE LOVES ME | none
H: Eleven pages, typed overnight. At the bottom of Nico's section, someone had added a line by hand in big, shaky, left-handed capitals. She will look like she loves me. I initialed it. I read every page twice. {p=0.5}
@s27 | zoom_in | ivy,nico | courthouse | close on the hands | Nico slips a too-large old square-cut emerald ring on a thin worn gold band onto Ivy's finger; it rotates loose; her other hand holds his wrist | ring too big, stone cloudy deep green; Nico splint RIGHT arm
> Soften slightly. Nico's line is almost shy.
H: The ring was his grandmother's, a square emerald, far too big. It slid around my finger. "Rosa had big hands," he said. "She made bread." {p=0.4}
H: On the courthouse steps, eight reporters and three cameras. Holloway had been tipped. Nico kissed me, slow and warm, the plaster along my spine. It was for the cameras. For three seconds I forgot to count anything, and I was angry about that all day. {p=0.3}
L: Starting tonight, Dottoressa, we're in love. {p=0.8}
@s28 | none | ivy,nico | bedroom | wide, a connecting door | A third-floor bedroom in an old Brooklyn brownstone; a brass bolt on Ivy's side of a connecting door with two crooked screws; Ivy tests it with her hand, the door half open to a dark room | brass bolt crooked left-handed screws; the emerald ring is on her left finger, loose, stone turned toward her palm; no chain yet
H: He had put a bolt on my side of the connecting door himself, at six that morning, left-handed. The screws were crooked. I tested it four times. The ring was so loose it kept turning to my palm. {p=0.5}
@s29 | zoom_in | ivy,nico | kitchen | close, kitchen island | Midnight at a marble kitchen island: Ivy re-stitching Nico's torn forearm sutures under a lamp, her pen in her teeth; he watches her face, not the needle | Nico splint RIGHT arm removed for the stitching; scar LEFT eyebrow; Ivy red bun, navy cardigan over scrubs
H: At midnight two of his stitches had torn. "Something heavy," he said. I numbed him and redid them at his mother's kitchen island, and I said it before I could stop. "You held the gun in your left hand." {p=0.5}
L: Don't. {p=0.6}
H: Not a denial. A door closing. I tied the knot. {p=1.0}

## CH08 | Leak
@s30 | zoom_in | ivy,priya | er | close, a gurney | Tuesday in the ER: Ivy cutting a black leather jacket off a young man, a hand-drawn calendar page slipped from its pocket; Priya at her shoulder | Ivy navy scrubs, chain with emerald ring under scrubs; red bun with pen
H: Tuesday, the ER. At work I couldn't wear a ring under gloves, so I'd threaded his grandmother's emerald onto a thin gold chain and tucked it under my scrubs. A wedding ring that lived against my breastbone. Joey, one of Nico's soldiers, rolled in stabbed, lung collapsing. I decompressed him and put in the tube myself while mobsters watched me do it. "Am I dead?" he asked. "Not on my shift." {p=0.4}
H: In his jacket I found my whole October. Every shift in blue ballpoint, small neat slanted handwriting I didn't know. And at the bottom, underlined twice: Level 2. Space 2-14. {p=0.5}
H: Joey said the man who sent him drove a car that smelled like anise. Only the hospital had my schedule. Dr. Bell had asked about my shifts that morning, almost casually. I told myself Bell asks everybody everything. {p=0.5}
@s31 | pan_left | ivy,priya,bell | er | medium, resident lounge | Dr. Bell, gray untrimmed beard with reading glasses on a cord, holds a coffee out to Ivy; Ivy's eyes on the cup | Bell gray beard, glasses on cord
H: A week later I set a trap: I told Bell I had a secret night shift Wednesday and would leave through level two. At 10:55 a man with a bandaged right hand waited at the exit, a hole in it from my shears. Tommy and Rocco boxed him in. I wouldn't let Nico kill him. I pinned a note to his shirt, FROM DR. MARLOWE, and had him left alive on the FBI's steps. {p=0.5}
@s32 | zoom_in | ivy,bell | er | close | Bell in a hallway taking off his glasses, shoulders collapsing, telling Ivy the truth with a sick teenager's photo visible on his phone lock screen | Bell gray beard; phone photo of a bald teenage boy in a gray beanie
H: I confronted Bell Thursday evening. He cried. His son Danny was seventeen and sick, and the treatment cost four hundred seventy thousand dollars. A man had asked about my schedule. Bell gave it. I gave him until noon to call the FBI. He asked for one more night. I said yes. I have thought about that yes every day since. {p=0.4}
@s33 | zoom_in | ivy,nico | bedroom | medium, connecting door | Ivy and Nico by the connecting door in lamplight, he lifts the pen out of her hair with his left hand, her hair falling; the closed door at her back | closed-door: kiss only, no skin shown
H: That night I was shaking, and he held me, and he pulled the pen out of my hair with his left hand. I kissed him. He kissed me back. I stopped. No cameras. "That one wasn't for anyone," I said. "Sleep," he said. "I'll wake you at eleven." {p=0.8}
@s34 | zoom_out | ivy,reyes | garage | wide, police tape | Friday dawn on level two: yellow tape around a silver Volvo three spaces from spot 2-14; Ivy at the tape; Reyes in a rain jacket beside her | Volvo silver; spot 2-14
H: At 6:41 Friday morning, Priya called. Dr. Bell was dead in his Volvo, three spaces from my spot. A note on his phone. Suicide. "Ivy, I'm sorry. I couldn't live with it. Tell Danny I tried." {p=0.4}
H: In three years Bell called me Ivy maybe four times, always when someone had just died. In writing, always Marlowe, even in his last text. The note was a lie, and whoever wrote it didn't know him. {p=0.4}
H: Only four people had known he'd talk to the FBI at noon. Nico. Tommy. Rocco. And Nico's uncle Sal. When I said that name aloud, Nico's eyes dropped to the top drawer of his desk, for as long as it takes to read a clock. {p=1.0}

## CH09 | Enzo
@s35 | zoom_in | ivy,enzo,theo | garden | medium, under a fig tree | Sunday lunch under a fig tree in the brownstone garden; Ivy kneeling on a bathroom floor upstairs is cut-to, a limp young man with gold curls on the tiles, a foil blister card beside him | Enzo gold curls, pale, thin
H: Sunday lunch under the fig. Enzo excused himself and didn't come back. I found him on his bathroom floor, blue, a foil card beside him. Oxycodone, thirty milligrams. I breathed for him. Two sprays of naloxone, three minutes apart. He gasped. I put the card in my cardigan pocket before the paramedics came. I only knew it mattered. {p=0.5}
@s36 | zoom_in | ivy,enzo | er | close, hospital bed | Enzo in a hospital bed with an oxygen cannula, gold curls flat with sweat, gripping Ivy's sleeve, Ivy leaning close | Enzo thin, pale; Ivy navy scrubs, cardigan
H: In the ER, Enzo grabbed my sleeve and told me, because he thought he was dying. "I shot him. I shot Frankie." {p=0.5}
H: He had followed Frankie into the garage and stood half a flight up the stairs, holding his breath. A shoe scraped. Frankie turned, and his gun came up. Enzo fired twice and didn't even decide to. Nico took the gun out of his hand and told him to go. {p=0.4}
H: Enzo was the light jacket on the stairs. He had stopped when he heard me ask for a tube. Then he kept going. I told him what he said in that bay stays with me. I didn't know yet what it would cost. {p=0.6}
@s37 | zoom_in | nico,frankie | garage | wide, from Nico's side | Nico walking up the dark ramp of the garage, Frankie crouched behind a silver sedan near the stairwell door crying with a gun loose in his hand, lights out above the stairs | white plaster splint RIGHT arm; Frankie good coat, gun in hand not raised
> Nico POV. Slow, flat, almost a confession. Let the end of it hang for a full beat.
L: Frankie called me at two minutes past four. By the stairs, where the lights are out. He was crouched behind a silver sedan, crying, a gun in his hand that he did not raise. {p=0.4}
